 was  easiest to find was the simple kind to  skin cleansing all but unfortunately it  looks like it's been discontinued there  is the qv bath oil in australia which  wasn't made to be a cleansing oil but it  works pretty well for that it does leave  behind a bit of residue though so you'll  want to follow up with a regular  cleanser the korean brand etude house  also has cleansing oils that sometimes  go on sale cleansing balms are solid  versions that are a bit easier to use  but they also tend to be a bit expensive  the cheapest options that i've tried and  loved are the ordinary squarelink  cleanser and the hamish all clean balm  if you're looking for a simpler  cleansing routine and you wear makeup or  waterproof sunscreen i would recommend a  micellar water you can use this as a  single product to remove makeup and  clean your skin simple have a really  good budget-friendly micellar water  bioderma is also fantastic i'd recommend  the sensor bio one over the sebium  because you can't use the sebium around  your eyes both of these have been  through a lot of clinical testing to  make sure they're really gentle and they  can be left on skin without rinsing for  most people without issues so it's  really good for after sports or if  you're just too tired to get to a sink  i'd also grab some reusable cotton pairs  to use with these cheaper disposable  cotton pads tend to be a bit rough on  skin i actually used to use some that  made me think that the micellar water  was too harsh for my skin for a long  time and yeah i'm not really that smart  i just play a smart person on tv most  brands of reusable cotton pads last for  ages you can also make some diy ones if  you're a bit crafty now on to acne  treatments there are a few common  over-the-counter options that are good  for dealing with acne but it's important  to remember as dr angeli mantra said in  our collaboration on food and acne  if you've given these over-the-counter  options a good bash and your acne  doesn't seem to be getting better and  this is an option that's available to  you it is a good idea to talk to a  doctor or dermatologist sometimes what  looks like acne is something else  sometimes acne comes from internal  causes that over-the-counter products  just can't really fix if your acne is  extending onto the rest of your body if  it's starting to scar or affect your  mental health these are good signs that  you should probably move on to  professional help let's start with the  most famous acne ingredient benzoyl  peroxide it is famous for a good reason  it is really effective at killing acne  bacteria and it does it in a way that  doesn't promote bacterial resistance in  other words as you're attacking the  bacteria the bacteria doesn't just  evolve and get even stronger which is  not a good situation benzoyl peroxide  also decreases clogged pores so it deals  with baby pimples that haven't become  full-blown pimples yet it is pretty  harsh and drying though so you'll  probably need a gentle cleanser and a  good moisturizer and it does tend to  work better when you use it all over  your face preventatively rather than as  a spot treatment after the pimple has  already well and truly left the station  my favorite type of benzoyl peroxide  product is a benzoyl peroxide cleanser  benzoyl peroxide is really annoying  because it does tend to stain things it  bleaches stuff it reacts with dyes and  fabric and it tends to  wander off your face onto things that  you really want to stay coloured so  things like pillowcases and towels and  shirts be really careful about what  you're using when you start using  benzoyl peroxide because chances are  your favorite shirt is going to get  ruined having the benzoyl peroxide in a  cleanser means that most of it will go  down the drain before that happens but  it is best to just be a bit careful wear  some old clothes at first if you're  using a benzoyl peroxide cleanser i  would recommend that you also have a  gentle cleanser as well because you  probably don't want to use the benzoyl  peroxide every day because yeah it can  really dry out your skin some popular  options are benzac ac wash which has 5  benzoyl peroxide and neutrogena clear  poor cleanser mask which has 3.5  when you use these you want to apply it  to your face and then wait about a  minute before you wash it away so that  that benzoyl peroxide has time to sink  into your skin and work these benzoyl  peroxide cleansers are also really handy  if you have acne on your body so you can  avoid stains on your clothes it can also  act as a deodorant if you use it on your  armpits because it kills the bacteria in  your armpits that causes the stink if  you want to use a leave-on benzoyl  peroxide product there's research  showing that 2.5 works as well as 10  with less irritation so go for the lower  percentage don't get sucked in by the  higher number and remember to put it on  all over so that it prevents future  pimples prevention is a lot easier than  treatment the second ingredient i  recommend for acne is salicylic acid  this is also known as beta hydroxy acid  and it acts as an exfoliant it helps  your dead skin cells shed more evenly  and that means it prevents clogged pores  and blackheads it keeps your pores  clearer and so they look less obvious  generally chemical exfoliants will just  smooth out your skin a bit it will  reduce some of that skin texture now the  problem with salicylic acid products is  that a lot of the good ones tend to be a  bit more expensive 0.5 to 2  is a good concentration for most people  the cheapest product i could find is the  revolution skincare 2 salicylic acid  tonic which is 20 for a 200 ml bottle my  favorite one actually isn't too bad it's  the paula's choice one which comes in a  mini 30 ml bottle for 15  which is surprisingly cheaper than the  inky list and revolution 30 mil ones for  australians at least  and i think it is just a much better  formula alternatively you could get the  118 ml bottle for 42. which works out to  be about one-third cheaper per ml the  way i use this is i pour a little bit  into my hand and then i spread it all  over my face with my fingers you can  also just rub your hands together and  then press it into your skin as well  some people like to apply these with a  cotton pad but to me it feels like i am  wasting product there is also azoleic  acid  azolic acid isn't my favorite ingredient  it seems to work for a lot of conditions  according to studies but it also just  doesn't really seem to be super  effective at any one thing  and the texture of azelaic acid products  is  kind of tacky and thick and gloopy but  azelaic acid is generally less  irritating than a lot of other active  ingredients so i thought i'd mention it  two options in australia are as clear  and phoenicia i do also want to mention  alpha hydroxy acids even though you have  an exfoliant in the beta hydroxy acid  the salicylic acid i've personally found  that with texture and whiteheads alpha  hydroxyaces tend to work a lot better my  budget-friendly picks for this would be  the ordinary's glycolic acetone which is  a little bit strong  it is a bit spicy on a lot of people's  skin so be careful with this don't use  it every day maybe mix it in with  something else so you don't use as much  there's also the ordinary's lactic acid  products these are a bit gentler lactic  acid in general is a bit gentler than  glycolic acid because the molecules are  larger so they don't get into the skin  as deeply onto a product that i really  wish i knew about when i was in high  school hydrocolloid pimple patches these  are little stickers that you stick onto  pimples and they soak up the liquid and  pass inside the pimples i've personally  found that they seem to work a little  bit better on inflamed pimples you can  also use them over intact pimples but i  personally feel like they work better  after i've stabbed the pimple with a  sterile lance i'm not sure if that's  just me being superstitious and i feel  like there's a tunnel so the liquid can  get out better but i have another video  on how to pop a pimple safely so check  that out if you  tend to pop your pimples and you can't  really stop the main thing i like about  these pimple patches is that they stop  you from picking out your pimples any  further plus sealing in the moisture  helps them heal a bit faster the most  budget-friendly option is to buy  something like a duradome bandage and  cut it up into little bits they can be a  bit chunky though and they can just sort  of leap off your face at awkward moments  if you move around too much like in the  middle of a sentence  so you probably only want to use these  at nighttime or if you're at home and  you're not really seeing anyone there  are also flatter versions that are  specifically made for pimples and these  are great because you can wear them  during the day and they're not super  obvious if your skin is raw it's really  hard to get makeup and concealer to sit  on it properly so if you have the right  color patch it disguises the pimple so  it's a lot less noticeable you can also  just go completely the other way and use  the pimple patches that look like stars  if that's more your style cosrx have  some really cheap ones there's also hero  cosmetics who have mighty patch and one  of the versions has tapered edges which  makes it blend in really nicely on your  face i think most countries also have  local versions of these pimple patches  in australia there's the very originally  named pimple patch  we are a simple people here so remember  if you've tried a few options and it  didn't really help or if your acne is  quite severe and causing scarring go see  a doctor you can also do this if you  just want to skip all this trial and  error and cut straight to the higher  risk higher reward options now sunscreen  if you've been on the internet you will  probably know that every skin care  person says sunscreen is super important  and it is it's really important for  protecting your skin from uv uv is  linked to skin cancer and premature  aging the uv can break down collagen  which is what keeps your skin bouncy so  it's a good idea to get into the habit  of wearing sunscreen every day if you're  going out into the sun for acne prone  skin sunscreen is especially good for  preventing post inflammatory  hyperpigmentation  this is where after your skin's been  traumatized such as after a pimple it  starts to just pump out extra brown  pigment and so you get this little patch  of brown you get a little dark spot and  this can take months to go away this  happens more if you have melanated skin  that is used to being good at producing  brown pigment the problem is that it can  be hard to get into the habit of wearing  sunscreen daily a lot of sunscreens feel  heavy and greasy so if you haven't used  a lot of skincare before then this can  be a lot to adjust to so the best  sunscreen for you is the sunscreen that  you'll wear consistently and at the  right amount and lots of studies have  shown that the nicer the texture of a  sunscreen the more likely it is that  you'll wear it consistently so generally  what you're looking for in a sunscreen  is firstly a suitable spf rating  generally over 30 is recommended and the  higher the number the more protection  you get if the amount of uv isn't that  high where you are spf 15 might be  enough you also want good uva protection  uva is a type of uv that's more strongly  linked to hyperpigmentation to get good  uva protection it's a bit more confusing  because depending on where in the world  you are it's labeled differently so  you're looking for broad spectrum or the  uva circle logo or pa with three or four  pluses or a high ppd number or more  stars in the boot star rating i have a  lot of other videos about sunscreens if  you want more details on how to choose  and use them but here are some popular  budget-friendly lightw