 with the standard super  budget-friendly bulk moisturizers that a  lot of people tend to recommend i'm  talking about things like cerave  cetaphil and qv which is like the  australian version of these and just saw  bling cream these tend to come in really  big pump bottles or in really big tubs  they do work for a lot of people so i'd  recommend giving them a try especially  if you have drier skin or if you live in  a cool dry climate but for me these tend  to feel a bit greasy they feel like  they're sitting on the surface of my  skin and not really sinking in and they  clog my pores so if you've tried these  and you don't like them but you still  feel like your skin needs a moisturizer  here are some lighter options it's  actually a bit difficult to find lighter  moisturizers that still hydrate well  from western drugstore brands but bondi  sands recently launched two moisturizers  that are really lightweight and  hydrating they're also fragrance free  neutrogena hydro boost also has  moisturizers that are really good  options but they are fragrance which can  be a problem if you're sensitive to  fragrance asian skincare brands tend to  have really good lightweight  moisturizers but  there are very few of them that are  consistently below 20 australian dollars  one that i've tried and seems to work  for a lot of people and stays around the  20 mark is the iunic cintella calming  cream this has centella which is great  for soothing irritated skin you can also  try slugging if you want this is where  you put a thick layer of vaseline over  your face while you sleep so you can  live the slug life  i'd be a little cautious about this for  acne prone skin because while petroleum  jelly by itself  isn't very good at clogging your pores  it can potentially help your other  products irritate more so proceed with  caution but it is a very cheap thing to  try that can work really well for dry  weather now if you're a bit of a skin  picker like i was when i was a teenager  