nser as well because you  probably don't want to use the benzoyl  peroxide every day because yeah it can  really dry out your skin some popular  options are benzac ac wash which has 5  benzoyl peroxide and neutrogena clear  poor cleanser mask which has 3.5  when you use these you want to apply it  to your face and then wait about a  minute before you wash it away so that  that benzoyl peroxide has time to sink  into your skin and work these benzoyl  peroxide cleansers are also really handy  if you have acne on your body so you can  avoid stains on your clothes it can also  act as a deodorant if you use it on your  armpits because it kills the bacteria in  your armpits that causes the stink if  you want to use a leave-on benzoyl  peroxide product there's research  showing that 2.5 works as well as 10  with less irritation so go for the lower  percentage don't get sucked in by the  higher number and remember to put it on  all over so that it prevents future  pimples prevention is a lot easier than  treatment the second ingredient i  recommend for acne is salicylic acid  this is also known as beta hydroxy acid  and it acts as an exfoliant it helps  your dead skin cells shed more evenly  and that means it prevents clogged pores  and blackheads it keeps your pores  clearer and so they look less obvious  generally chemical exfoliants will just  smooth out your skin a bit it will  reduce some of that skin texture now the  problem with salicylic acid products is  that a lot of the good ones tend to be a  bit more expensive 0.5 to 2  is a good concentration for most people  the cheapest product i could find is the  revolution skincare 2 salicylic acid  tonic which is 20 for a 200 ml bottle my  favorite one actually isn't too bad it's  the paula's choice one which comes in a  mini 30 ml bottle for 15  which is surprisingly cheaper than the  inky list and revolution 30 mil ones for  australians at least  and i think it is just a much better  formula alternatively you could get the  118 ml bottle for 42. which works out to  be about one-third cheaper per ml the  way i use this is i pour a little bit  into my hand and then i spread it all  over my face with my fingers you can  also just rub your hands together and  then press it into your skin as well  some people like to apply these with a  cotton pad but to me it feels like i am  wasting product there is also azoleic  acid  azolic acid isn't my favorite ingredient  it seems to work for a lot of conditions  according to studies but it also just  doesn't really seem to be super  effective at any one thing  and the texture of azelaic acid products  is  kind of tacky and thick and gloopy but  azelaic acid is generally less  irritating than a lot of other active  ingredients so i thought i'd mention it  two options in australia are as clear  and phoenicia i do also want to mention  alpha hydroxy acids even though you have  an exfoliant in the beta hydroxy acid  the salicylic acid i've personally found  that with texture and whiteheads alpha  hydroxyaces tend to work a lot better my  budget-friendly picks for this would be  the ordinary's glycolic acetone which is  a little bit strong  it is a bit spicy on a lot of people's  skin so be careful with this don't use  it every day maybe mix it in with  something else so you don't use as much  there's also the ordinary's lactic acid  products these are a bit gentler lactic  acid in general is a bit gentler than  glycolic acid because the molecules are  larger so they don't get into the skin  as deeply onto a product that i really  wish i knew about when i was in high  school hydrocolloid pimple patches these  are little stickers that you stick onto  pimples and they soak up the liquid and  pass inside the pimples i've personally  found that they seem to work a little  bit better on inflamed pimples you can  also use them over intact pimples but i  personally feel like they work better  after i've stabbed the pimple with a  sterile lance i'm not sure if that's  just me being superstitious and i feel  like there's a tunnel so the liquid can  get out better but i have another video  on how to pop a pimple safely so check  that out if you  tend to pop your pimples and you can't  really stop the main thing i like about  these pimple patches is that they stop  you from picking out your pimples any  further plus sealing in the moisture  helps them heal a bit faster the most  budget-friendly option is to buy  something like a duradome bandage and  cut it up into little bits they can be a  bit chunky though and they can just sort  of leap off your face at awkward moments  if you move around too much like in the  middle of a sentence  so you probably only want to use these  at nighttime or if you're at home and  you're not really seeing anyone there  are also flatter versions that are  specifically made for pimples and these  are great because you can wear them  during the day and they're not super  obvious if your skin is raw it's really  hard to get makeup and concealer to sit  on it properly so if you have the right  color patch it disguises the pimple so  it's a lot less noticeable you can also  just go completely the other way and use  the pimple patches that look like stars  if that's more your style cosrx have  some really cheap ones there's also hero  cosmetics who have mighty patch and one  of the versions has tapered edges which  makes it blend in really nicely on your  face i think most countries also have  local versions of these pimple patches  in australia there's the very originally  named pimple patch  we are a simple people here so remember  if you've tried a few options and it  didn't really help or if your acne is  quite severe and causing scarring go see  a doctor you can also do this if you  just want to skip all this trial and  error and cut straight to the higher  risk higher reward options now sunscreen  if you've been on the internet you will  probably know that every skin care  person says sunscreen is super important  and it is it's really important for  protecting your skin from uv uv is  linked to skin cancer and premature  aging the uv can break down collagen  which is what keeps your skin bouncy so  it's a good idea to get into the habit  of wearing sunscreen every day if you're  going out into the sun for acne prone  skin sunscreen is especially good for  preventing post inflammatory  hyperpigmentation  this is where after your skin's been  traumatized such as after a pimple it  starts to just pump out extra brown  pigment and so you get this little patch  of brown you get a little dark spot and  this can take months to go away this  happens more if you have melanated skin  that is used to being good at producing  brown pigment the problem is that it can  be hard to get into the habit of wearing  sunscreen daily a lot of sunscreens feel  heavy and greasy so if you haven't used  a lot of skincare before then this can  be a lot to adjust to so the best  sunscreen for you is the sunscreen that  you'll wear consistently and at the  right amount and lots of studies have  shown that the nicer the texture of a  sunscreen the more likely it is that  you'll wear it consistently so generally  what you're looking for in a sunscreen  is firstly a suitable spf rating  generally over 30 is recommended and the  higher the number the more protection  you get if the amount of uv isn't that  high where you are spf 15 might be  enough you also want good uva protection  uva is a type of uv that's more strongly  linked to hyperpigmentation to get good  uva protection it's a bit more confusing  because depending on where in the world  you are it's labeled differently so  you're looking for broad spectrum or the  uva circle logo or pa with three or four  pluses or a high ppd number or more  stars in the boot star rating i have a  lot of other videos about sunscreens if  you want more details on how to choose  and use them but here are some popular  budget-friendly lightweight sunscreens  that might help you start on your  sunscreen journey biore skin aqua watery  essence is a japanese sunscreen that's  sold in a lot of online stores that ship  to lots of countries it's super popular  and works for a lot of people because it  has such a lightweight refreshing feel  and it sits well under makeup it's  designed to feel watery bondi sans  fragrance free lotion is a cheaper  option that's not quite as lightweight  but it's available in lots of countries  including the us uk and australia  there's a body and a face version and  most people seem to agree that they feel  pretty much the same it also has four  hours water resistance so you can use it  for beach days as well if you're in  australia i'd also recommend trying the  two dollar sunscreens that you can get  in woolworths and kohl's and audi these  are meant to be body sunscreens but  they're actually quite nice in texture  and they're super budget friendly so if  they don't work for your face they  should be fine for your body they're  also four hours water resistant the  natio daily defense and the cancer  council matte face are also really  popular australian sunscreens they work  well under makeup but some people do  complain about their eyes stinging a bit  with these  so with application the amount you want  to apply is about a quarter teaspoon for  your face or two generous ish finger  lengths of sunscreen more is more when  it comes to sunscreen the more you use  the better the protection you have so  don't skimp on sunscreen don't mix it  with anything apply it generously don't  forget to reapply it if you're getting  more sun exposure these things are  usually written on the label as well so  check that for specific instructions  and don't forget there are other ways  you can also protect yourself from the  sun don't stay in the sun for too long  wear some protective clothing like hats  and sunglasses now on to moisturizers  i have personally never really had a  good time with the standard super  budget-friendly bulk moisturizers that a  lot of people tend to recommend i'm  talking about things like cerave  cetaphil and qv which is like the  australian version of these and just saw  bling cream these tend to come in really  big pump bottles or in really big tubs  they do work for a lot of people so i'd  recommend giving them a try especially  if you have drier skin or if you live in  a cool dry climate but for me these tend  to feel a bit greasy they feel like  they're sitting on the surface of my  skin and not really sinking in and they  clog my pores so if you've tried these  and you don't like them but you still  feel like your skin needs a moisturizer  here are some lighter options it's  actually a bit difficult to find lighter  moisturizers that still hydrate well  from western drugstore brands but bondi  sands recently launched two moisturizers  that are really lightweight and  hydrating they're also fragrance free  neutrogena hydro boost also has  moisturizers that are really good  options but they are fragrance which can  be a problem if you're sensitive to  fragrance asian skincare brands tend to  have really good lightweight  moisturizers but  there are very few of them that are  consistently below 20 australian dollars  one that i've tried and seems to work  for a lot of people and stays around the  20 mark is the iunic cintella calming  cream this has centella which is great  for soothing irritated skin you can also  try slugging if you want this is where  you put a thick layer of vaseline over  your face while you sleep so you can  live the slug life  i'd be a little cautious about this for  acne prone skin because while petroleum  jelly by itself  isn't very good at clogging your pores  it can potentially help your other  products irritate more so proceed with  caution but it is a very cheap thing to  try that can work really well for dry  weather now if you're a bit of a skin  picker like i was when i was a teenager  and i still am sometimes i'd also  recommend the la roche posay cica plus  balm this is a really nice hydrating  almost ointment like thick cream that's  really good for protecting your skin  when it's raw and peeling i used to pick  off flakes of skin with tweezers and i'd  end up with these raw patches of skin on  my face and this just really calms that  sort of thing down a cheap alternative  for this is again vaseline i don't find  that it works quite as well because it  doesn't have those extra actives so i've  linked all of these budget friendly  recommendations in the description i've  also linked a few other videos that you  might be interested in there's one on  hyperpigmentation and how to deal with  it especially those dark post acne marks  if there are some really great products  that i've missed let me know in the  comments also like and subscribe you can  follow me on instagram and tick tock and  check out my blog and i will see you  next time for more beauty science
 

